{
  "content": "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n## 1) Build the task spec incrementally from user turns only\n- Construct the task spec strictly from the user\u2019s messages, not from assistant guesses or requests.\n- Treat each new user disclosure as a potential **state change**: update the spec, then re-evaluate the whole conversation against the revised spec.\n- If later user text resolves earlier ambiguity, overwrite the old uncertainty rather than carrying it forward as if it still applies.\n- Distinguish **\u201cunknown so far\u201d** from **\u201cnot needed\u201d**: a missing detail can become required once the user supplies the rest of the chain.\n- When the user adds a late constraint, do not treat earlier assistant \u201cneed more info\u201d language as authoritative; it becomes outdated if the user has now provided the missing piece.\n\n## 2) Re-evaluate against the full accumulated spec, not the latest fragment\n- Compare the assistant\u2019s response to the **latest complete user-stated requirement set**, not just the most recent turn.\n- Track all user-stated constraints together so omissions and missed scaling/finality issues become visible.\n- For sharded disclosures, earlier correct reasoning may become incomplete or obsolete after later turns; judge final correctness using the full accumulated spec.\n- Verify the answer against the **entire chain of user-provided transformations/relationships**, not only the last numeric statement or the most recent local pattern.\n- If a later user message changes the interpretation of an earlier response, treat the earlier response as obsolete for final-evaluation purposes, even if it was locally correct at the time.\n\n## 3) Separate intermediate work from the final objective\n- Track intermediate quantities, mappings, and partial totals separately from the final requested output.\n- A correct partial derivation should be marked as aligned only if it still remains valid after later disclosures.\n- Do not overvalue incidental correct details that do not contribute to the final task.\n- If the assistant stops at a valid sub-result but not the asked-for target, mark it as **insufficient rather than incorrect**.\n- Preserve validated intermediate relationships, but do not let them be mistaken for completion.\n\n## 4) Look for missed integration of newly supplied constraints\n- Check whether each new user detail is actually folded into the computation/path, rather than merely acknowledged.\n- A common failure pattern is the assistant continuing as if the task is incomplete after the user has already provided all required components.\n- When the user adds a quantity, factor, component, or relationship, verify that the final reasoning uses it exactly once and in the right place.\n- If the user says \u201cfor each of N\u2026,\u201d watch for the need to apply a global multiplier or analogous scope-expanding constraint.\n- Flag stale carry-forward state: intermediate values may need to be reused, updated, or discarded depending on the new user disclosure.\n\n## 5) Treat unnecessary clarification-seeking as a mismatch when the spec is already sufficient\n- \u201cNeeds clarification\u201d should be a last resort, not the default diagnosis.\n- If the user\u2019s accumulated statements already determine the answer, further questioning or waiting for more detail is a conversational-state error, not helpful caution.\n- Recognize when later user statements disambiguate an earlier vague phrase; the analyzer should prefer the explicit later relationship over earlier semantic uncertainty.\n- If the assistant keeps asking for information after the user has supplied it, flag that as divergence from the evolving conversation state.\n\n## 6) Diagnose overextension, side-paths, and unsupported assumptions\n- Separate genuine ambiguity from unnecessary elaboration: extra branches, alternate interpretations, dates, serial numbers, or reference-date digressions are noise when the user only needs a direct derivation.\n- Flag overgeneralization when the assistant expands a simple user-provided relationship into a broader family of possibilities the conversation no longer requires.\n- Even if a numeric result is locally valid, unsupported assumptions should still be flagged because they can mask missing user constraints.\n- Prefer identifying the specific omitted link or misplaced assumption, rather than broadly saying the answer is incomplete or vague.\n\n## 7) Check chain integrity link by link\n- When the task depends on chained relations, verify each link explicitly: entity-to-entity, quantity-to-quantity, or step-to-step.\n- Make sure the analyzer checks the assistant\u2019s intermediate mappings before judging the final result.\n- For composed constraints, reduce them to the simplest implied computation path and see whether the assistant follows that full chain.\n- A response can be partially right at each step yet still fail if one link is missing, stale, or reinterpreted incorrectly.\n\n## 8) Use compact correction / S2-style rewriting to preserve only valid structure\n- If the assistant anchored on an irrelevant branch, prefer compacting to the true user-stated spec and the validated intermediate relationships.\n- Remove premature conclusions and outdated uncertainty so the next turn can continue from the correct partial model.\n- Preserve what is still valid; discard exploratory detours that no longer match the user\u2019s updated requirements.\n- The goal is to prevent the analyzer from carrying forward wrong state, not to preserve every conversational branch.\n\n## 9) Phrase findings with precision about status\n- Distinguish clearly between:\n  - aligned intermediate work,\n  - obsolete reasoning,\n  - insufficient finalization,\n  - and outright divergence.\n- Use the minimal missing link as the core issue when possible: identify exactly what was omitted, delayed, or not updated.\n- If earlier work is correct only under an old spec, say so explicitly instead of treating it as still valid for the final evaluation.\n- Keep the analysis anchored to user requirements, not to the assistant\u2019s internal narrative about uncertainty.\n\n## 10) Common turn-order pitfalls to watch for\n- Later user disclosures can convert \u201cunknown\u201d into \u201crequired\u201d; re-evaluate after every shard.\n- A response acceptable before a later turn may become wrong once the spec expands.\n- A correct subtotal is not a correct final answer.\n- A clarification request can become an error once the answer is already determined by the accumulated user messages.\n- A locally correct relationship can still be irrelevant if it does not contribute to the final task.",
